<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolko I of Opole</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1258–<a href="/years/1313">1313</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolko I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> was a Silesian duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a> who ruled Opole from 1281. A supporter of the Czech kings, he extended his influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>. On his death in 1313 his lands were divided among his sons, creating separate duchies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niemodlin">Niemodlin</a> and Opole.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bolko was the third son of Władysław, Duke of Opole-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a>, and of Euphemia, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic">Władysław Odonic</a>, Duke of Greater Poland. Around 1277 his father named him co-ruler despite his place in the family. On his father's death he and his brother Casimir inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> together, while their brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> and Przemysław took Racibórz. The joint rule lasted until 1284, when Bolko kept Opole and Casimir received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Unlike his brothers, Bolko worked closely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-probus">Henry IV Probus</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, backing him in his long conflict with Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-ii-zaremba">Thomas II Zaremba</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek II the Black</a> died in 1288, Bolko joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-glogow">Henry III of Głogów</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemko-of-scinawa">Przemko of Ścinawa</a> in an attack on Siewierz on 26 February 1289. The battle was extremely bloody; Przemko was killed, and Bolko was seriously wounded and captured by Władysław I the Elbow-high, who released him a year later for a large ransom.</p><p>After Henry IV died in 1290, Bolko allied with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a>, agreeing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> on 17 January 1291, with Mieszko and Casimir, to fight any enemy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a>. He attended Wenceslaus's coronation in 1297, represented him in 1298 at the meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> that chose Albert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> as German king, and was made Starost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> after Wenceslaus became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1300. The extinction of the Přemyslid dynasty in 1306 broke the alliance. In 1312, during the rebellion of the wójt Albert, Bolko came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> but failed to take Wawel Castle, and on returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> he imprisoned Albert.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bolko was known for his generosity to the Church, supporting the Cistercian monastery at Jemielnica and building the chapel of St. Anna in the Franciscan church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, and he granted Magdeburg Law to many places in his duchy. He died on 14 May 1313 and was buried in the Franciscan church. His wife Agnes, of unknown origin, bore him three sons: Bolesław the Elder, Bolko II and Albert.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolko I of Opole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the death of Duke <b>Bolko I of Opole</b> in 1313, his lands were divided among his sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-niemodlin">Duchy of Niemodlin</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Bolko I of Opole</b> died in 1313, his son Albert received Strzelce (Strehlitz), east of Opole, as his share of the Duchy of Opole.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-strzelce">Duchy of Strzelce</a></li></ul></section>
